import{c as t,Q as n,j as r,m as o}from"./chunks/framework.BqPkuuVc.js";const c=JSON.parse('{"title":"USB printers on Windows","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"user/reference/usb-printer-on-windows.md","filePath":"user/reference/usb-printer-on-windows.md"}'),i={name:"user/reference/usb-printer-on-windows.md"};function s(a,e,h,d,p,g){return n(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="usb-printers-on-windows" tabindex="-1">USB printers on Windows <a class="header-anchor" href="#usb-printers-on-windows" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;USB printers on Windows&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>Audience:</strong> Anyone plugging a USB thermal printer into a Windows laptop that runs Manager in the browser — or wondering whether their label printer can go on one.</p><p>On Windows, a USB printer needs its driver changed once before the browser can print on it. This page covers the symptom, why it happens, how to change the driver with Zadig, and how to tell it worked.</p><blockquote><p><strong>Applies to:</strong> a printer added as <strong>Into this computer</strong> — a USB printer that the laptop&#39;s browser prints on — on <strong>Windows</strong>. It is not about one make of printer: Windows does this to almost every USB printer. A printer plugged into the <strong>server</strong> and added <strong>Into the server</strong> is printed through Windows&#39; own driver and needs none of this — see <a href="#do-i-need-this">Do I need this?</a> below.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p><strong>Check the printer first: it must understand ESC/POS.</strong> Zadig lets the browser <em>reach</em> a USB printer; it does not let the browser <em>print</em> on one that needs its maker&#39;s driver. A laptop&#39;s browser sends ESC/POS, the language of thermal printers, and nothing else. A <strong>Brother QL</strong> label printer (QL-570, QL-700, QL-800…), a <strong>DYMO LabelWriter</strong> or an office printer will accept the bytes, report success on <strong>Test print</strong>, and print nothing. Do not run Zadig for one of those: plug it into the server, keep Windows&#39; driver, and add it <strong>Into the server</strong> — see <a href="./../running-the-event/splits-printing#which-printers-a-laptop-can-print-on">Which printers a laptop can print on</a>.</p></blockquote><h2 id="the-symptom" tabindex="-1">The symptom <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-symptom" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The symptom&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Test print</strong> — or any splits print or label for that printer — fails with:</p><blockquote><p><em>Windows has its own driver on this USB printer, so the browser cannot use it. Switch the printer&#39;s driver to WinUSB with Zadig; removing the printer from Windows&#39; printers is not enough.</em></p></blockquote><p>Older versions of Manager said <em>Windows is using this printer through its own driver</em> instead, or — wrongly — <em>A USB printer needs the secure (https://) link to meshO</em>, even on a page already opened over https. All three mean the same thing, and the fix is the one below.</p><h2 id="why-it-happens" tabindex="-1">Why it happens <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-it-happens" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why it happens&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When a USB printer is plugged in, Windows recognises it as a printer and attaches its own driver to it — <strong>USB Printing Support</strong> (<code>usbprint</code>). It does this before anything else gets a look, whether or not you ever install the printer in <strong>Settings</strong>.</p><p>The browser talks to USB devices through <strong>WebUSB</strong>, and on Windows WebUSB can only open a device whose driver is <strong>WinUSB</strong>. With <code>usbprint</code> holding the printer, the browser asks to open it and Windows answers &quot;access denied&quot;.</p><p>So it is the combination — <strong>a USB printer, on Windows, printed by the browser</strong> — not your particular printer, and not the browser or the https link. Chrome and Edge behave the same. (Firefox can&#39;t print on a USB printer at all, on any system: it has no WebUSB.)</p><blockquote><p><strong>Removing the printer from Windows&#39; printers does not fix it.</strong> <strong>Settings → Bluetooth &amp; devices → Printers &amp; scanners → Remove</strong> deletes Windows&#39; print queue for the printer. The <code>usbprint</code> driver underneath is still attached the next time it is plugged in. Removing the queue is still worth doing — see step 1 below — but changing the driver is the step that matters.</p></blockquote><h2 id="change-the-driver-to-winusb-with-zadig" tabindex="-1">Change the driver to WinUSB with Zadig <a class="header-anchor" href="#change-the-driver-to-winusb-with-zadig" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Change the driver to WinUSB with Zadig&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Do this once on each Windows laptop, with the printer plugged in and switched on.</p><ol><li><strong>Remove Windows&#39; printer for it, if there is one.</strong> <strong>Settings → Bluetooth &amp; devices → Printers &amp; scanners</strong>, open the printer (it may be called <strong>POS-80</strong>, <strong>Generic / Text Only</strong> or the printer&#39;s model) and choose <strong>Remove</strong>.</li><li><strong>Download Zadig</strong> from <a href="https://zadig.akeo.ie" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://zadig.akeo.ie</a> and run it. There is nothing to install; say <strong>Yes</strong> to the administrator prompt.</li><li>Choose <strong>Options → List All Devices</strong>.</li><li>In the dropdown, choose the printer. It is listed by its own name (<strong>POS-80</strong>, <strong>TM-T20III</strong>…) or as <strong>USB Printing Support</strong>. If it appears twice, choose the one marked <strong>(Interface 0)</strong>.</li><li>The box on the left shows its current driver — usually <strong>usbprint</strong>. Set the box on the right to <strong>WinUSB</strong> and click <strong>Replace Driver</strong>. It takes up to a minute.</li><li><strong>Unplug the printer and plug it back in.</strong></li><li><strong>Close the browser completely</strong> and open Manager again at its https link.</li><li>Go to <strong>Settings → Printing → Printers</strong> and run <strong>Test print</strong> on the printer. If it says the printer is not plugged in, open the printer and <strong>Choose the USB printer</strong> again — the browser sees it as a new device now that its driver has changed.</li></ol><h3 id="check-it-worked" tabindex="-1">Check it worked <a class="header-anchor" href="#check-it-worked" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Check it worked&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Open <strong>Device Manager</strong>. The printer is now listed under <strong>Universal Serial Bus devices</strong>, not under <strong>USB Printing Support</strong> or <strong>Printers</strong>. In Zadig, choosing it again shows <strong>WinUSB</strong> in the left-hand box.</p><h3 id="what-changes-afterwards" tabindex="-1">What changes afterwards <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-changes-afterwards" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What changes afterwards&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Only browsers can print on it.</strong> Word, the Windows print dialog and other programs no longer see it as a printer. That is what the laptop&#39;s USB printer is for in Manager, so usually nothing is lost.</li><li><strong>It stays changed.</strong> Windows remembers the driver for that printer on that USB port; the next event needs nothing. Plugging it into a <em>different</em> USB port can bring <code>usbprint</code> back — run Zadig again if the message returns.</li><li><strong>To undo it</strong> — to let Windows and its programs print on the printer again, or because it turned out to be a printer the browser cannot print on. Zadig itself cannot put Windows&#39; driver back; Device Manager can: <ol><li>In <strong>Device Manager</strong>, find the printer under <strong>Universal Serial Bus devices</strong>, right-click it and choose <strong>Uninstall device</strong>.</li><li>Tick <strong>Attempt to remove the driver for this device</strong>, then confirm.</li><li>Unplug the printer and plug it back in. Windows attaches <strong>USB Printing Support</strong> again.</li><li>For a printer with its own maker&#39;s driver, such as a Brother QL, install that driver again if Windows does not list the printer under <strong>Printers &amp; scanners</strong>.</li></ol></li></ul><h3 id="tested-but-nothing-printed" tabindex="-1">Tested, but nothing printed? <a class="header-anchor" href="#tested-but-nothing-printed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tested, but nothing printed?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>If <strong>Test print</strong> now says it worked but no paper comes out, the driver change has done its job and the printer does not understand what the browser sends. That is a printer that is not ESC/POS — see the note at the top of this page — and no driver setting on the laptop changes it. Undo the change as above, and plug the printer into the server instead.</p><h2 id="do-i-need-this" tabindex="-1">Do I need this? <a class="header-anchor" href="#do-i-need-this" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Do I need this?&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A USB thermal printer (ESC/POS) on a Windows laptop, added as <em>Into this computer</em>:</strong> yes.</li><li><strong>A Brother QL, DYMO or other label printer that needs its own driver:</strong> no — it cannot be printed on from a laptop at all. Put it on the server, with its own driver.</li><li><strong>A printer plugged into the server&#39;s computer, added <em>Into the server</em>:</strong> no. The server prints through Windows&#39; own driver, the way any program does, so leave the driver alone. Changing it to WinUSB would stop the server printing on it. On the server&#39;s own computer this is usually the simpler choice anyway: the server prints on it even when no Manager page is open, and the <strong>Add a printer</strong> dialog marks it as the best choice there.</li><li><strong>A network printer:</strong> no. It is reached by its address and has no USB driver.</li><li><strong>A Mac:</strong> no. macOS does not attach a driver to a USB printer, so the browser can open it as it is. If a print fails there with <em>The USB printer could not print it</em>, another program has the printer open — close it and try again.</li></ul><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./../running-the-event/splits-printing">Printing splits</a> — adding printers, and where each computer&#39;s prints go.</li><li><a href="./https-and-certificates">HTTPS and certificates</a> — the secure link a laptop&#39;s browser needs before it can print on a USB printer at all.</li><li><a href="./troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> — general &quot;it won&#39;t connect&quot; help.</li></ul>',27)])])}const u=t(i,[["render",s]]);export{c as __pageData,u as default};
